            },
            "createdOn": 1752914478,
            "author": {
                "name": "Prithvi Pai",
                "email": "prithvi24pai@gmail.com",
                "username": "PrithviPai24"
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "oem/nvidia/bootstrap-credentials-oem-cmds.cpp",
                    "line": 25,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vernon Mauery",
                        "email": "vernon.mauery@gmail.com",
                        "username": "vmauery"
                    },
                    "message": "generally, IPMI returns values LSB first. It looks like this code is forcing the uint16_t to be returned MSB first. If you want LSB first, just return a uint16_t type and the packer will do all the work for you."
                },
                {
                    "file": "oem/nvidia/bootstrap-credentials-oem-cmds.cpp",
                    "line": 25,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Prithvi Pai",
                        "email": "prithvi24pai@gmail.com",
                        "username": "PrithviPai24"
                    },
                    "message": "We would need MSB first. Hence we are forcing for that."
                },
                {
                    "file": "oem/nvidia/bootstrap-credentials-oem-cmds.cpp",
                    "line": 31,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vernon Mauery",
                        "email": "vernon.mauery@gmail.com",
                        "username": "vmauery"
                    },
                    "message": "Is type ever going to be something other than 1 or 2? If not, using a map seems excessive. A simple if statement for two entries is more efficient in code size and runtime, as well as easier to read.\n\n```\nconstexpr uint8_t descriptorVendorId = 1;\nconstexpr uint8_t descriptorProductId = 2;\n\nif (type == descriptorVendorId)\n{\n    return ipmi::successResponse(usbVendorId);\n}\nelse if (type == descriptorProductId)\n{\n    return ipmi::successResponse(usbProductId);\n}\nreturn ipmi::responseInvalidieldRequest();\n```"
                },
                {
                    "file": "oem/nvidia/bootstrap-credentials-oem-cmds.cpp",
                    "line": 31,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Prithvi Pai",
                        "email": "prithvi24pai@gmail.com",
                        "username": "PrithviPai24"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "oem/nvidia/bootstrap-credentials-oem-cmds.cpp",
                    "line": 45,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vernon Mauery",
                        "email": "vernon.mauery@gmail.com",
                        "username": "vmauery"
                    },
                    "message": "I didn't see this IPMI function referenced in the design document that you pointed at. I only saw the credentials bootstrap and certificate fingerprint functions. There was mention of a usb descriptor, but it was more involved, getting more parts of the descriptor than just the deviceID and productID."
                },
                {
                    "file": "oem/nvidia/bootstrap-credentials-oem-cmds.cpp",
                    "line": 45,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Prithvi Pai",
                        "email": "prithvi24pai@gmail.com",
                        "username": "PrithviPai24"
                    },
                    "message": "I updated the commit message. Upcoming PRs will have the support for other device descriptors."
                }
            ],
